WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.476
Picture packing your lunch every morning from a written list.

2
00:00:06.476 --> 00:00:09.015
Cook the rice, which takes forty minutes.

3
00:00:09.015 --> 00:00:09.881
Add the curry.

4
00:00:09.881 --> 00:00:10.748
Pack a napkin.

5
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:14.619
One morning you want a blue napkin instead of a white one.

6
00:00:14.619 --> 00:00:16.697
And your list has a strange rule.

7
00:00:16.697 --> 00:00:20.160
Change any step, and you must redo every step after it.

8
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:23.410
Fine, as long as the napkin line comes last.

9
00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:29.021
But if it came first, you would cook the rice again, forty minutes, just to swap a napkin.

10
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:31.409
Nobody would write a list like that.

11
00:00:31.409 --> 00:00:34.068
And yet, in software, we do it all the time.

12
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.913
Docker's recipe for an image is a file called a Dockerfile.

13
00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:47.600
It is read from the top, one instruction per line, and each line that changes files becomes a layer, like the tiers from last time.

14
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:49.960
Start from a Python base image.

15
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.880
Work in a folder called app.

16
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.898
Copy in every file of our app, hello-devops.

17
00:00:54.898 --> 00:00:57.024
Then pip installs its packages.

18
00:00:57.024 --> 00:00:58.533
That is the slow step.

19
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:00.874
The first build runs every step.

20
00:01:00.874 --> 00:01:03.952
The pip install alone takes about ten seconds.

21
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:07.589
Docker keeps every layer it builds, in a build cache.

22
00:01:07.589 --> 00:01:12.820
Next time, it reuses a step if that step, and everything before it, is unchanged.

23
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:17.364
Now you change one comment in app.py, and build again.

24
00:01:17.364 --> 00:01:18.966
Which steps run again?

25
00:01:18.966 --> 00:01:20.131
Pause and guess.

26
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:23.982
The first two are reused.

27
00:01:23.982 --> 00:01:27.938
But COPY dot dot copied every file, and one of them changed.

28
00:01:27.938 --> 00:01:29.323
So that layer is new.

29
00:01:29.323 --> 00:01:34.136
And every layer after it is rebuilt too, including the whole pip install.

30
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:36.964
Ten seconds of installing, for a comment.

31
00:01:36.964 --> 00:01:39.372
That is the blue napkin, packed first.

32
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.950
Is Docker being lazy?

33
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:42.621
Let's be fair to the rule.

34
00:01:42.621 --> 00:01:47.764
Each layer holds the changes one step made, on top of the exact layers below it.

35
00:01:47.764 --> 00:01:55.349
If a lower layer changed, Docker cannot know whether pip install would give the same result, without running it again.

36
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:59.567
So we keep the same lines, and change only the order.

37
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:02.081
Copy the list of packages on its own.

38
00:02:02.081 --> 00:02:02.964
Install them.

39
00:02:02.964 --> 00:02:04.527
And copy the code last.

40
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:07.317
Edit the same comment, and build again.

41
00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:10.100
The three steps above the code say cached.

42
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.737
Only the last small layer runs, and the whole build takes 0.3 seconds.

43
00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:20.623
Roughly thirty times faster than a fresh install, and we did not write a single new line.

44
00:02:20.623 --> 00:02:22.212
We just moved the napkin.

45
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:23.859
So here is the rule.

46
00:02:23.859 --> 00:02:26.283
Put what changes least at the top.

47
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:27.637
Copy the code last.

48
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:30.659
One honest limit: what counts as changed.

49
00:02:30.659 --> 00:02:34.882
COPY checks the contents of files, not when they were touched.

50
00:02:34.882 --> 00:02:37.266
RUN only compares the command text.

51
00:02:37.266 --> 00:02:42.443
So RUN apt-get update stays cached for months, even when new packages exist.

52
00:02:43.267 --> 00:02:46.927
One more line in the recipe decides how the box stops.

53
00:02:46.927 --> 00:02:54.112
When you run docker stop, Docker sends a polite message, SIGTERM, to the container's first process, PID 1.

54
00:02:54.112 --> 00:02:55.875
Then it waits ten seconds.

55
00:02:55.875 --> 00:03:00.552
If the app is still running, it sends SIGKILL, which ends it at once.

56
00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:03.486
Here is CMD written plainly, as words.

57
00:03:03.486 --> 00:03:08.280
Docker runs it through a shell, a program that reads command lines.

58
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:15.507
So the shell, sh, becomes PID 1, and gunicorn, the web server that runs hello-devops, sits behind it.

59
00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:16.893
Pause and guess.

60
00:03:16.893 --> 00:03:19.139
How long does docker stop take?

61
00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.434
The message arrives at the door.

62
00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:26.276
The shell takes it, and does not pass it on.

63
00:03:26.276 --> 00:03:28.149
gunicorn never hears a thing.

64
00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:33.008
So Docker waits out the full ten seconds, and then kills it.

65
00:03:33.008 --> 00:03:36.721
We measured it: 10.2 seconds, and exit code 137.

66
00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:40.380
A shell that keeps your mail and never forwards it.

67
00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.624
So the point is: the app itself has to be PID 1.

68
00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:47.730
Now write CMD as a list in square brackets.

69
00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.821
That is called exec form.

70
00:03:49.821 --> 00:03:53.668
No shell in between: gunicorn itself is PID 1.

71
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.407
docker stop again.

72
00:03:55.407 --> 00:04:00.095
gunicorn gets the message, finishes politely, and exits.

73
00:04:00.095 --> 00:04:02.773
0.6 seconds, and exit code zero.

74
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.272
One honest catch.

75
00:04:04.272 --> 00:04:06.592
Exec form is only half the fix.

76
00:04:06.592 --> 00:04:09.585
The app must also handle SIGTERM itself.

77
00:04:09.585 --> 00:04:10.632
gunicorn does.

78
00:04:10.632 --> 00:04:18.713
A bare Python script does not, and as PID 1 it waited out the full ten seconds in our test, just like sleep.

79
00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:21.271
So the moral of the story.

80
00:04:21.271 --> 00:04:26.817
Put the slow, stable steps first and the code last, so an edit rebuilds in a blink.

81
00:04:26.817 --> 00:04:32.631
And write CMD as a list, so the app itself hears the polite message, and stops in time.

82
00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:33.795
Quick check.

83
00:04:33.795 --> 00:04:38.120
A Dockerfile copies every file, then runs pip install.

84
00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:39.402
You edit app.py.

85
00:04:39.402 --> 00:04:40.924
Does pip run again?

86
00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:43.444
Yes.

87
00:04:43.444 --> 00:04:47.011
A changed layer rebuilds every layer after it.

88
00:04:47.011 --> 00:04:49.105
Move the napkin to the end.
